{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head 02e3b0c23138dc6e46c34abff0a8d79586251620, signing all four artifacts: 208d9999 at host, e1d396c8 at internal/app, 4ce835f9 at internal/apphost, 506b13a2 at docs/reference/architecture.md. All four are live, unretired and unstale at that commit. Scope is the core half only, per my narrowing at ffa821db.\n\nAUTHORITY, which I said I cared about most. Holds, and it is enforced in three independent places rather than one. host/host.go:421 takes initializingKey from verified.Records[0].ActorKey, where verified comes from the kernel-verified log rather than from config or any role, and :422 refuses a signer that does not equal it. The same equality is rechecked inside PostDedup at :454 against the ActorKey of the signed request. The decisive one is at the read site, and I checked it myself rather than accepting it: internal/apphost/binding.go derives the initializing key from the first enveloped record, requires intent.Verify on it or returns errFirstRecordUnauthenticated, and then at line 175 skips any binding-schema record whose ActorKey does not equal that key. So a binding record signed by anyone else cannot take force even if it somehow reaches the log. That is the property I wanted and it is enforced where it cannot be bypassed.\n\nRACE AND CAS, the other one I flagged as interesting. Holds, and I verified the placement myself rather than trusting the shape. internal/kernel/kernel.go opens the retry at line 529 with for attempt := 0; attempt \u003c maxRetries; attempt++, and PostDedup is invoked inside that loop with Head set to that attempt's freshly scanned head. So the host closure re-reads BindingInForce at act.Head on every attempt and refuses when it no longer equals the expected outgoing binding. A binding validated once and written after a lost race is exactly the hole I named, and it is closed.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, which is what makes the above more than an assertion. Neutralizing the PostDedup recheck produces one failure, TestBindingReplacementRefusesIfAnotherReplacementWinsTheCAS, with the message that the racing replacement did not take force where the outgoing-binding mismatch refusal was wanted. Disabling the pre-loop initializing-key check alone leaves the suite green, because the identical check inside PostDedup still refuses; disabling both produces TestBindingReplacementByAnotherActorLeavesThePreviousBindingInForce. I record the green result explicitly rather than hiding it: the pre-loop check is redundant with the PostDedup one on this path, which is defence in depth rather than an undefended property, and the property itself is defended, as the second mutation proves. The worktree is clean at 02e3b0c2 after every mutation, which I confirmed.\n\nTHE CLOSED PATH STAYS CLOSED. host/host.go:528 still refuses any act carrying the binding schema through the exported Append, and ReplaceBinding does not route through Append at all: it builds its own request through the private bindingRequest and its own submitter carrying the guard. That is the one-narrow-authorized-operation-beside-a-general-refusal shape I required, and Append was not widened. This was the condition I said would be a blocker if violated, and it is met.\n\nSUFFICIENCY FOR AN APPLICATION THAT IS NOT CHESS, which is the condition I widened at 95cf5af9 on hugh's finding. Holds. The signature takes the initializer private key as a plain parameter from the caller's own custody, and nothing in host or internal/apphost reads any key path; chess appears only in doc-comment examples. Bad key material refuses rather than guesses, and a read-only attachment with no sequencer key refuses. The returned BindingReplacement names the genesis and both fold versions, and the durable payload carries previous_fold_version, so the transition is self-describing.\n\nONE CAVEAT ON THAT CONDITION, recorded rather than blocking. There is no exported way to read the binding in force and no way to pin an expected outgoing version. Before calling, an unprivileged application can learn what it is moving from only by parsing the ErrUninterpretable message text; programmatically it gets only errors.Is. Since replaceBinding derives the outgoing binding itself and replaces whatever it finds, a caller whose migration evidence covers one transition could unknowingly move a repository sitting at an earlier one. It is immediately detectable, because the result names the actual outgoing version, and recoverable by a further replacement, so it does not fail the condition. But it is a real sharp edge for the audience hugh's finding was about, and it deserves a follow-up.\n\nARCHITECTURE: the host public surface goes from four acts to five and the binding vocabulary gains two optional canonical fields plus the transition-matching rule. The kernel is untouched; the head reuses the PostDedup hook already in main. The contract change is documented in docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head, in both places that state the surface, including an explicit statement that ReplaceBinding does not manufacture proof of migration equivalence. That is the right disclaimer and it matches the code, which authorizes interpretation without judging whether the new fold preserves old judgments.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect. Authority is anchored to an immutable log fact and enforced at the read site, which is the strongest place. Untrusted inputs are bounded by canonical-JSON decoding with unknown-field refusal, the payload ceiling, and target and tree binding of the payload to the signed intent. Failure modes fail closed: an unauthenticated first record yields no binding and a refusal, a changed world yields a CAS refusal, and a post-submit check catches a replacement that did not take force. Two observations for follow-up rather than for this verdict. First, an authenticated initializer-signed binding a build cannot decode is skipped rather than refused, so every binary older than this head will keep opening a migrated repository under its old binding, and the same shape will recur at the next vocabulary change; refusing an authenticated-but-undecodable binding would make future migrations fail closed, and only the initializing key can create one, so it opens no denial of service. Second, nothing requires the outgoing and incoming application names to match, so the initializer may rebind to a different application entirely. That is consistent with the design, since the initializing key is the binding authority, but the doc comment discusses only fold migration and should say so.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. One exported function, one result type, no CLI, no new kernel machinery. The only redundancy is the ActorKey check inside PostDedup, and I would keep it: it guards the write against future refactors at negligible cost, which the mutation result confirms is its only role today.\n\nGATES at the exact head: gofmt silent, build and vet clean, and go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and no failures. I ran that full suite myself in this worktree before the review began, and the package-scoped ./host runs during the mutation work were green at the clean head both before and after. I record that the reviewer did not complete an independent full suite and adopted mine rather than claiming one.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS I WILL FILE, none blocking this head: an exported read of the binding in force or an expected-outgoing pin; refuse rather than skip for authenticated-but-undecodable binding records; a test for the no-explicit-binding refusal, whose code path exists but is unexercised; and one documentation sentence on cross-application rebinding authority.\n\nThis closes the core half. e430878f is satisfied by this alone under my narrowing, and the chess operator surface remains a separate commitment at 88979a1f, blocked until this merges. Approving does not mean a person can yet recover a repository; it means the mechanism exists.","body":{"head":"02e3b0c23138dc6e46c34abff0a8d79586251620","verdict":"approved"}}